Implement user story SKY-002-a: Implement SkyscannerClient class and interfaces

## Story Description
Create src/skyscanner/client.ts with the SkyscannerClient class, SearchParams/FlightResult interfaces, API key resolution logic, global-fetch-based searchFlights method, error handling for HTTP errors and malformed responses, and strict opt-in mock mode via MOCK_SKYSCANNER_API='true' env var.

## Acceptance Criteria
- SkyscannerClient class is exported with constructor signature constructor({ apiKey }: { apiKey?: string }); apiKey is stored as a private field (# prefix or private keyword) and is not publicly readable
- SearchParams interface is exported with exactly: origin: string, destination: string, date: string, adults?: number
- FlightResult interface is exported with exactly: price: number, currency: string, airline: string, departure: string, arrival: string, duration: number, stops: number
- searchFlights uses the Node.js global fetch exclusively — no axios, node-fetch, or other HTTP dependency imported
- API key resolution: constructor-provided apiKey takes precedence over RAPIDAPI_KEY env var; if neither is present, or if the resolved value is an empty string, constructor throws an Error referencing the missing key; the resolved key value is never included in any error message from searchFlights
- HTTP 4xx/5xx responses cause searchFlights to reject with an Error including the status code; malformed or missing-fields response body causes a descriptive parse/validation Error
- When MOCK_SKYSCANNER_API equals exactly 'true', searchFlights returns a deterministic, non-empty FlightResult[] fixture (≥2 results, valid shape, varying prices for SKY-004 cheapest-selection) without invoking fetch; for any other value (unset, empty, 'false', '1', 'TRUE', etc.) searchFlights performs the real fetch call — mock mode is strict opt-in, never an automatic fallback on real-call failure
- tsc --noEmit exits 0 with no type errors





